\section{Where the brand comes from}{

\code{brand = NULL} finds the brand the way Quarto does. Inside a Quarto render
it is the brand Quarto resolved for the document (a project \verb{_brand.yml},
or \verb{brand:} in \verb{_quarto.yml} or the front matter, including light and dark
brands; \code{brand: false} means no brand). Outside Quarto it is the file named
by the \code{BRAND_YML_PATH} environment variable, or the first \verb{_brand.yml}
found in the working directory or a parent of it (also in a \verb{brand/} or
\verb{_brand/} subdirectory). Reading a file needs the brand.yml package.
}

\section{Using the brand for every plot}{

Set \code{options(vellumplot.brand = TRUE)} (in a document's setup chunk, say) to
theme every plot that has no theme of its own with \code{theme_brand()}, found
as for \code{brand = NULL}. \code{options(vellumplot.brand = "path/to/_brand.yml")}
names the file. A plot with an explicit theme (\code{theme_bw()}, a
\code{theme_brand()} of its own) keeps it; \code{\link[=theme]{theme()}} tweaks on an unthemed plot
apply on top of the brand. The first branded plot of a session says which
file it used. The option is off by default, so existing documents do not
change their look. Add \code{options(vellumplot.brand.fetch_fonts = TRUE)} to
fetch missing brand fonts there, as \code{fetch_fonts = TRUE} does.
}

\section{Fonts}{

A brand font that is not installed falls back to the default family, with
a warning. \code{fetch_fonts = TRUE} installs it for the session first: a
\code{google} or \code{bunny} font is downloaded with \code{\link[systemfonts:require_font]{systemfonts::require_font()}}
(this needs the network), and a \code{file} font is registered from the brand's
directory with \code{\link[systemfonts:add_fonts]{systemfonts::add_fonts()}}. Fonts registered this way are
used for drawing and measuring text in every output format.
}

